Step 2 — User starts a protected action

The user clicks a protected action such as login, sign up, password reset, token request, or file upload.
This is usually the right point to render Turnstile in an SPA.
Step 3 — Turnstile runs in the SPA
SPA UI on app.example.com renders Turnstile

The browser completes the challenge and receives a token named cf-turnstile-response.
Turnstile runs in the browser, not in WAF and not in the API.
Step 4 — SPA sends the protected request to the API
SPA api.example.com/api/auth/login→
POST /api/auth/login

The request includes the business data plus the Turnstile token.
Step 5 — Cloudflare evaluates the API request first

Before the request reaches origin, Cloudflare WAF and Bot Management evaluate hostname, path, bot score, verified bot status, and any rate-limiting logic.
If the request is obviously malicious, Cloudflare blocks or challenges it before it reaches the API server.
Step 6 — The API receives the request

If Cloudflare allows the request through, the API origin receives both the request payload and the cf-turnstile-response token.
Step 7 — The API verifies the Turnstile token
API server Cloudflare Siteverify→

The API sends the secret key and the token to the Siteverify endpoint.
This validation is mandatory because tokens can be forged, expire after 5 minutes, and are single-use.
Step 8 — The API decides success or failure

If verification fails, return 400 or 403 and deny the protected action. If verification succeeds, continue the protected action and then create the application’s normal session or auth token.
The Turnstile token itself is not your reusable session.
Step 9 — Future API calls use normal app authentication

After the protected action succeeds, the SPA uses your normal session cookie or application-issued auth token for subsequent API calls.
